### 4.4 Results

Using this information we can apply an ensemble level subtraction to measure the correlated signal distribution of \(z_{\rm g}\). Within each jet \(p_{\rm T}\) bin for 0-10% and 10-20% centrality, the ME \(z_{\rm g}\) distribution was scaled by the extracted combinatorial contribution and then subtracted from the SE \(z_{\rm g}\) distribution. While the subtraction is done in bins of centrality of width 10%, the final measured distribution is reported for 0-20% for statistical reasons, as a semi-inclusive measurement is extremely statistically limiting. In Figure 4.16, the combinatorial subtracted \(z_{\rm g}\) distributions are shown for three jet \(p_{\rm T}\) ranges and are compared to fast simulated PYTHIA embedded into minimum-bias Run14 data. Only the highest \(p_{\rm T}\) bin is reported as a STAR preliminary, which is the \(p_{\rm T,jet}\) least sensitive to combinatorial jets and the subtraction method. Before combinatorial subtraction, this \(p_{\rm T,jet}\) bin contained 5% contamination for 0-10% and sub 1% for 10-20% events. When compared to smeared PYTHIA p+p there was found to be no modification of the \(z_{\rm g}\) distribution in this bin. The two lower \(p_{\rm T,jet}\) bins do show a change in distribution when comparing to the smeared PYTHIA embedding, however it should be noted that these two bins are sensitive to the details of the ensemble-level combinatorial jet subtraction. While the systematic uncertainties due to variations of \(\rho\) and \(f_{\rm ME}\) are expected to capture possible misestimations of the combinatorial jet contribution, the modification within these two bins is seemingly consistent with contamination due to combinatorial jets. Most notably, while the highest \(\rho\) and \(f_{\rm ME}\) are expected to be compatible with the \(\rho\) and \(f_{\rm ME}\) distributions, the \(\rho\) and \(f_{\rm ME}\) distributions are expected to be compatible with the \(\rho\) and \(f_{\rm ME}\) distributions.

Figure 4.16: Measured combinatorial subtracted \(z_{\rm g}\) distributions compared to fast simulated PYTHIA embedded into minimum-bias Au+Au Collisions for 0-20% centrality. Only highest jet \(p_{\rm T}\) bin has been shown as a STAR preliminary.

\(p_{\rm T,jet}\) bin has agreement with the \(z_{\rm g}\) = 0 bin, there is a large difference for this bin in 10 \(<\)\(p_{\rm T,jet}\)\(<\) 15 GeV/\(c\) jets. As previously mentioned, combinatorial jets rarely fail Soft Drop, and contamination leftover from the ensemble-level subtraction would yield such a suppression in this bin. This behavior warranted extra investigation, and thus only the data for the high \(p_{\rm T,jet}\) bin was requested and approved as a preliminary for STAR.

### 4.5 Further Study

In order to further investigate the performance of the combinatorial subtraction, a study was performed by embedding PYTHIA events into Mixed Events. This was done to probe the rate of contamination of combinatorial jets within a given \(p_{\rm T,jet}\) bin in order to compare to. PYTHIA8 events were generated with the same high-\(p_{\rm T}\) neutral requirement as in the Same Event class, requiring a photon or \(\pi^{0}\) with \(p_{\rm T}\)\(>\) 9 GeV/\(c\). These particle-level PYTHIA events were then embedded into Mixed Events (recreated with a different seed than the ME in the analysis), sampling the SE distributions in ZDC coincidence rate and event \(V_{Z}\). No smearing was applied to the PYTHIA events because the study is testing the performance of the subtraction method as a whole and is not necessarily concerned with the details of Fast Simulation. These embedded events were named "Hybrid Events" and in the study replaced the Same Event class for the analysis steps. The same procedure for the analysis is followed, with each HE event sampling the corresponding ME bin.

First, the median-\(p_{\rm T}\) density \(\rho\) was calculated for HE and ME events, with the choice of the number of hard jets excluded in the calculation explored. A comparison of the \(\rho\) distributions for 3 choices can be found in Figure 4.17. Just like in the Same Event case, the best agreement can be found with excluding a single hard jet in the \(\rho\) calculation for Hybrid Events. This is determined when the ratio under the peak of the distribution being flat and approximately agreeing with unity. The change in the choice of the number of excluded jets in the \(\rho\) calculation has similar behavior to what was found in the actual analysis with the SE and ME \(\rho\) comparison. It should be noted that the structure of the enhancement at high \(\rho\) is in part due to the effect of embedding additional particles into the ME to create the HE, causing an increase into the max multiplicity of the events. Ultimately the same definition of \(\rho\) in the SE class was used for HE, excluding a single hard jet from the \(\rho\) calculation.

Figure 4.17: Median \(p_{\mathrm{T}}\)-density \(\rho\) in Hybrid and Mixed events for different choices of the number of hardest jets excluding in the calculation for HE.

Following the \(\rho\) calculation, jets were reconstructed for HE and ME within the recoil region \(|\phi_{\rm trig}-\phi_{\rm jet}|>\frac{3\pi}{4}\), where the trigger axis was not a high-\(E_{\rm T}\) tower but instead the trigger photon or \(\pi^{0}\) within the PYTHIA event. The jets were area-based subtracted, and the ME jets were scaled down by the \(f_{\rm ME}\) scale factor as determined by the region dominated by combinatorial jets. The comparison between the HE and scaled ME per-trigger recoil jet yields can be found in Figure 4.18.

The scale factor was found to be \(f_{\rm ME}=0.806\), which is noticeably lower than \(f_{ME}\) as found in the analysis. This smaller \(f_{\rm ME}\) is expected because no efficiency or smearing was applied to the PYTHIA events, causing an enhanced per-trigger yield of high-\(p_{\rm T}\) jets than one would expect in actual data. Due to the integral of both the HE and ME (before scaling) being the same, an increase in per-trigger yields at the high \(p_{\rm T,jet}\) region for HE events would cause a decrease in the per-trigger yields at the scaling region. Even with the difference in the scaling factor, the

Figure 4.18: Per-trigger recoil jet yields for Hybrid and Mixed Events for 0-10% centrality. Mixed Event distribution scaled down by \(f_{\rm ME}\). The ratio of the distributions is shown in the bottom panel.

gross features of the distributions remain the same, with the ratio in the combinatorial dominated region being flat at unity.

Using the per-trigger recoil yields for HE and the scaled ME, the combinatorial subtracted HE yields can be obtained in the same manner as in the analysis. While in the analysis there is no ability to check the purity of the subtracted yields, with this embedding study a comparison to the expected actual yields within a given \(p_{\mathrm{T,jet}}\) bin can be explored. The jets reconstructed in the HE events are checked if they are matched to a PYTHIA particle-level reconstructed jet. The HE jets are considered matched if their jet axes are within \(\Delta R<\) 0.4 of a PYTHIA jet. However, it was found that there is a sensitivity to the minimum jet \(p_{\mathrm{T}}\) used to cluster the PYTHIA jets. A comparison of the combinatorial subtracted per-trigger yields in the HE study to the amount of jets matched to PYTHIA with varying minimum PYTHIA \(p_{\mathrm{T,jet}}\) can be found in Figure 4.19.

When

