## Chapter 5 Discussion and Outlook

One of the strengths of the semi-inclusive approach is minimally discriminating on a jet-by-jet basis which allows reconstructing jets to a near-zero \(p_{\rm T,jet}\). However care has to be taken when interpreting the results of such low-\(p_{\rm T}\) objects. Normally a measurement is unfolded to account for the fluctuation of the background, and jets are analyzed at meaningful \(p_{\rm T,jet}\) values. While jet measurements can be made without unfolding and making comparisons to a smeared or embedded reference, they are usually done so with cuts that select biased but hard jets. The very lack of discrimination on a jet-by-jet basis, while ideal for the method, causes issues making comparisons without unfolding. The two low-\(p_{\rm T,jet}\) bins in this \(z_{\rm g}\) analysis likely have low-\(p_{\rm T}\) contributions below the \(p_{\rm T}\) threshold one would normally consider to be a jet. These contributions are not a flaw, they are correlated signal and the method treats them as such. However, it is not meaningful to measure substructure of such jets in \(p_{\rm T,jet}^{\rm reco}\) bins that contain such contributions. In the end, only the highest \(p_{\rm T,jet}^{\rm reco}\) bin in the analysis, 20 \(<p_{\rm T,jet}^{\rm reco}<\) 25 GeV/\(c\) was released as a preliminary, which is high enough \(p_{\rm T,jet}^{\rm reco}\) such that there is not a significant contribution of very low-\(p_{\rm T}\) yield. In this bin, it was found there was no modification relative to the embedded and smeared PYHTIA p+p comparison. It is important to note that despite \(z_{\rm g}\) not being modified, these jets are known to be quenched, as a previous measurement by STAR has shown that thesemi-inclusive jet spectra in central Au+Au collisions to be highly suppressed [69]. This lack of modification of \(z_{\rm g}\) is in agreement with the previous \(z_{\rm g}\) measurement at STAR[42]. The measurement in this thesis presents a different selection of jets with different analysis steps and supports the same conclusion. It should be noted that both measurements are not unfolded and are compared to at the detector level, which leaves room for further measurements. However, as stated previously, \(z_{\rm g}\) is extremely difficult to unfold due to the presence of non-diagonal elements in the response matrix. With current background subtraction techniques, it is not feasible to unfold without reducing the contribution from the background such as going to small jet \(R\) or increasing the \(z_{\rm cut}\), as was done in the ALICE measurement of \(z_{\rm g}\). While such a measurement would be interesting, restricting the phase space in order to be able to unfold causes the observable to deviate away from its intended purpose of trying to capture the kinematics of the first hard splitting.

The details of extending the semi-inclusive approach to jet substructure observables has been explored in this thesis. Using the semi-inclusive approach to measure a substructure observable is an attractive method, it selects a sample of jets while avoiding the normal surface and jet-biases that reduce the potential to find modification of jet substructure. While \(z_{\rm g}\) is an interesting observable in its own right, for the sake of a semi-inclusive measurement, it is not ideal. The method requires an observable that can be unfolded back to the particle-level distributions in order to account for the minimal selection criteria imposed. It should be noted that a downside to the analysis method is that it is extremely statistics hungry, requiring a high-\(p_{\rm T}\) trigger much greater than the online high tower trigger greatly reduces that amount of usable events. While in this thesis work there was no differentiation between photon and \(\pi^{0}\) triggers, with sufficient statistics the difference between the triggers can be used to select different sample of jets with expected differences in path length in the medium. A possible future measurement using a semi-inclusive approach to measuring jet substructure will greatly benefit from the increased statistics expected from future STAR Au+Au runs in 2023 and 2025.

